---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const isothermCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '1383/x', label: 'T = 166 K (cool isotherm)', color: '#1d4ed8' },
    { expr: '2494/x', label: 'T = 300 K (room)', color: '#1f6f54', width: 2.2 },
    { expr: '3326/x', label: 'T = 400 K (hot)', color: '#b42318' },
  ],
  x: { min: 0.02, max: 0.14, label: 'volume V (m³)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 130000, label: 'pressure P (Pa)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 350,
};

const thStatesBuckets = {
  "prompt": "The kinetic particle model explains all three states. File each property:",
  "buckets": [
    "Solid",
    "Liquid",
    "Gas"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "particles vibrate about fixed positions",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "strong bonds hold the shape",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "particles stay touching but free to slide",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "fixed volume, no fixed shape",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "particles far apart, fast and random until they collide",
      "bucket": 2
    },
    {
      "label": "fills whatever container it gets",
      "bucket": 2
    }
  ],
  "why": "One picture covers the ladder: vibrating solids, sliding liquids, flying gases — and melting, boiling, condensing and freezing are just transitions along it."
};

const thBoyleCloze = {
  "text": "Boyle's law worked example: 0.10 m³ of air at 100 kPa compressed to 0.04 m³ gives P₂ = %%0%% kPa. And beware the scale trap: doubling a Celsius temperature does %%1%% double the pressure — only %%2%% may be doubled.",
  "answers": [
    [
      "250"
    ],
    [
      "not"
    ],
    [
      "kelvins",
      "kelvin"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "P₁V₁ = P₂V₂; and 20 °C is 293 K while 40 °C is only 313 K."
};

const thLakePredict = {
  "q": "A lake in mid-winter, air at −10 °C, ice on top. What is the water temperature at the bottom of the lake?",
  "choices": [
    "−10 °C — it matches the air",
    "0 °C — the whole lake sits at freezing",
    "About 4 °C — water's density peak sinks below the ice"
  ],
  "answer": 2,
  "why": "Water is the beautiful exception: it contracts as it warms from 0 to 4 °C, so 4 °C water is densest and sinks. Lakes freeze top-down, insulating the fish below."
};

const thKettleEstimate = {
  "prompt": "A 3 kW kettle heats 1.5 kg of water from 20 °C to 100 °C. Roughly how long does it run?",
  "unit": "minutes",
  "answer": 2.8,
  "factor": 1.6,
  "why": "ΔE = mc·Δθ = 1.5 × 4,186 × 80 ≈ 500 kJ; at 3,000 J/s that is about 170 s — 2.8 minutes.",
  "context": "Steel's c ≈ 490 is the contrast: a small iron pan screams hot while the full kettle is lukewarm on the same hob."
};

const thBreezeOrder = {
  "prompt": "Put the daytime sea breeze in order:",
  "items": [
    "Land heats faster than the sea",
    "Air over the land warms, expands and becomes less dense",
    "Buoyancy floats the warm air upward",
    "Cooler sea air sinks and flows in to replace it",
    "The loop keeps circulating — the breeze you feel on your face"
  ],
  "why": "Convection moves energy by moving matter: heat does not rise — the heated fluid rises. The same loop runs thunderstorm updrafts and the mantle's slow conveyor."
};

const thBoilMatch = {
  "prompt": "Related, not identical — match each property to its process:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "happens at any temperature",
      "right": "evaporation"
    },
    {
      "left": "happens throughout the liquid, in bubbles",
      "right": "boiling"
    },
    {
      "left": "loses the fastest molecules — which is why the puddle cools",
      "right": "evaporation"
    },
    {
      "left": "vapour pressure matches atmospheric pressure",
      "right": "boiling"
    }
  ],
  "why": "Both cool what remains; only boiling demands the full latent-heat rate at one temperature — and near Everest's summit, that temperature drops to body heat."
};

const thSteamJudge = {
  "statements": [
    {
      "s": "Steam at 100 °C scalds worse than boiling water at 100 °C.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "Condensing on skin, steam deposits its latent heat — about 2,257 kJ/kg on top of the hot-liquid transfer."
    },
    {
      "s": "Evaporating sweat carries away enough energy to cool a 70 kg human by over 10 °C.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "1.5 kg of sweat ≈ 3,600 kJ — and humidity slows evaporation, which is what makes humid heat lethal."
    },
    {
      "s": "Ice cools a drink only by being cold.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "Melting adds another 334 kJ/kg on top of the temperature difference — the latent half of the cooling."
    },
    {
      "s": "While water boils, its temperature keeps rising.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "The thermometer freezes at 100 °C — the energy goes into breaking intermolecular bonds, not into jiggling."
    }
  ]
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/intermediate/thermal-physics">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This intermediate lesson builds directly on the foundational lessons on energy
    conservation (energy stored and transferred, the joule) and Newton's laws (force and
    pressure). If you have not met the energy-accounting picture yet, work through that
    lesson first: heat is one of its transfer mechanisms, and the first law of
    thermodynamics is energy conservation wearing thermal clothes. Otherwise nothing is
    assumed beyond multiplication and division; every formula is unpacked as it arrives.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>state the difference between temperature and heat, and explain why it matters immediately;</li>
    <li>use the kinetic particle model to explain the three states, changes of state and Brownian motion;</li>
    <li>derive the gas laws from molecular bombardment and calculate with the Kelvin scale correctly;</li>
    <li>calculate energy changes with specific heat capacity and latent heat, and explain water's anomalous role;</li>
    <li>analyse conduction, convection and radiation, and audit real designs — flasks, insulation, astronaut suits — against all three.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Temperature is not heat. <strong>Temperature</strong> measures the average kinetic energy
    of a substance's particles — how vigorously they jiggle; <strong>heat</strong> is energy
    <em>in transit</em> because of a temperature difference. The distinction matters
    immediately: a bathtub at 30 °C contains far more thermal energy than a match flame, but
    only the flame has the higher temperature, and heat flows from <em>hot to cold</em>,
    regardless of quantity. This chapter builds the particle model that explains both, derives
    the gas laws from molecular bombardment, and then follows thermal energy on its journeys —
    through solids, liquids and empty space — with the numbers that engineering and weather
    actually run on.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Boyle's law at three temperatures — PV = nRT drawn as hyperbolas; hotter gas, higher curve" widget="plotter" config={isothermCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> trace one curve from left to right: as volume shrinks,
    pressure climbs steeply — the inverse relationship of Boyle's law. Then fix your eye on
    one volume and read straight up: at the same volume, the hotter curve sits higher,
    because faster molecules strike the walls harder and more often. Nothing on the diagram
    crosses zero pressure at any positive volume — absolute zero is where the model says the
    bombardment stops.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each hyperbola is one mole of ideal gas at a fixed temperature (P = RT/V, R = 8.314
    J/mol·K). Compressing along any curve raises pressure; jumping curves (heating at fixed
    volume) raises it too. Real engines live on exactly these diagrams.
  </p>

  <h2>States of matter: one model, three behaviours</h2>
  <p>
    The kinetic particle model explains the three states with one picture. In a
    <strong>solid</strong>, particles are packed in a regular array, vibrating about fixed
    positions — strong bonds hold the shape. In a <strong>liquid</strong>, particles remain
    touching but free to slide — fixed volume, no fixed shape. In a <strong>gas</strong>,
    particles are far apart, moving fast and random until they collide — no fixed volume or
    shape, filling whatever container they get. Melting, boiling, condensing and freezing are
    transitions along this ladder: adding energy weakens and finally breaks the arrangements
    (which is why a substance's temperature <em>stops rising</em> during a change of state —
    the energy is going into the bonds, a thread we pick up below). The model earned its place
    by explaining <strong>Brownian motion</strong>: pollen grains in water jitter randomly
    because invisible molecules bombard them unevenly — visible evidence of invisible motion,
    and Einstein's 1905 analysis of it clinched the reality of atoms.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="file the properties — one model, three behaviours" widget="sort-buckets" config={thStatesBuckets} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> one particle model — vibrating solids, sliding liquids, flying
    gases — explains the three states and every change between them, and Brownian motion gave
    it visible evidence. Now the model earns its keep quantitatively: the gas laws.
  </p>

  <h2>Gases and the absolute scale of temperature</h2>
  <p>
    Gas pressure is molecular bombardment: quadrillions of collisions per second on each square
    centimetre of wall, each transferring a tiny impulse. That picture predicts the gas laws
    directly. <strong>Boyle's law</strong> (P ∝ 1/V at fixed temperature): squeeze to half the
    volume and molecules hit the walls twice as often, so pressure doubles — the widget's
    hyperbolas, and the reason a pump gets harder as you push. Worked example: 0.10 m³ of air
    at 100 kPa compressed to 0.04 m³ gives P₂ = P₁V₁/V₂ = 100 × 0.10/0.04 = 250 kPa.
    <strong>Pressure law</strong> (P ∝ T at fixed volume): hotter molecules move faster and
    strike harder and more often. <strong>Charles's law</strong> (V ∝ T at fixed pressure):
    warmer gas pushes harder, so it must expand to keep the balance. Plot any of these toward
    zero and the lines converge on one temperature: <strong>−273.15 °C</strong>, absolute zero,
    where the model says molecular motion is minimal. That convergence defines the
    <strong>Kelvin scale</strong> (K = °C + 273.15), the scale the gas laws demand: always
    convert. Room temperature is 293 K; doubling "temperature" means doubling kelvins, never
    degrees Celsius. The three laws collapse into one statement, the ideal gas equation
    <strong>pV = nRT</strong> — and it works because temperature <em>is</em> average molecular
    kinetic energy: at room temperature a nitrogen molecule zips at about 500 m/s (the speed
    of a rifle bullet), colliding billions of times per second.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="fill the blanks — gas-law arithmetic and the kelvin rule" widget="cloze" config={thBoyleCloze} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "if 20 °C becomes 40 °C, the temperature has doubled, so
  the pressure doubles too." Only kelvins may be doubled: 20 °C is 293 K and 40 °C is 313 K —
  a rise of about 7%, not 100%. The Celsius zero point sits at the freezing point of water,
  an arbitrary place on the scale; zero kelvin is where the gas laws converge. Always
  convert before any gas-law arithmetic.</p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "metal feels colder, so the metal object <em>is</em>
  colder." At room temperature the steel spoon and the wooden board are at exactly the same
  temperature. Metal <em>feels</em> colder because it conducts heat out of your hand far
  faster — the sensation of temperature is really a sensation of energy flow rate. The same
  physics in reverse: a stone floor and a carpet at the same 20 °C, and only one of them is
  pleasant to walk on barefoot.</p>

  <h2>Thermal expansion: matter's looseness under heat</h2>
  <p>
    Heat a solid and its particles vibrate harder; because the interaction is asymmetric
    (pushing apart is easier than pulling together), the average spacing grows — the solid
    <strong>expands</strong>. The effect is small but engineering-scale: a 100 m steel bridge
    grows about 12 mm for a 10 °C rise (steel: ~12 × 10⁻⁶ per °C), which is why bridges have
    expansion joints and gaps, rails are laid with tolerances (until a heatwave buckles a
    track — a genuine annual news story), and buildings sit on sliding bearings. Exploit it
    and you get the <strong>bimetallic strip</strong>: two metals with different expansion
    rates bonded together bend when heated, snapping a thermostat's switch — the cheap
    temperature-sensor inside irons and kettles.
  </p>
  <p>
    Water is the beautiful exception. Between 0 and 4 °C it <strong>contracts as it
    warms</strong>, reaching maximum density at 4 °C; ice floats because freezing locks
    molecules into an open structure <em>less</em> dense than the liquid. Consequences run
    deep: lakes freeze from the top down (4 °C water sinks, insulating the fish below),
    icebergs sail, and frozen pipes burst as expanding water finds no room.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="bet first — the winter lake's bottom" widget="predict" config={thLakePredict} />

  <h2>Specific heat capacity: the energy price of a degree</h2>
  <p>
    Raising temperature costs energy, and different substances charge different prices. The
    <strong>specific heat capacity</strong> c is the energy to raise 1 kg by 1 °C; the working
    relation is <strong>ΔE = mc·Δθ</strong>. Worked example: a kettle with 1.5 kg of water
    from 20 °C to 100 °C needs E = 1.5 × 4,186 × 80 ≈ 500 kJ. A 3 kW kettle delivers 3,000
    J/s, so it runs about 2.8 minutes — check any kettle against this and the physics passes.
    Steel (c ≈ 490) heats and cools faster than water; a small iron pan can be screaming hot
    while its full kettle neighbour is lukewarm on the same hob.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="call the magnitude — your kettle's running time" widget="estimate" config={thKettleEstimate} />
  <p>
    Water's c = 4,186 J/kg·K is among the highest of any common substance, and civilisation
    is built on it. Oceans store summer heat and release it in winter (maritime climates are
    mild; continental ones extreme); car cooling systems and central heating circulate water
    rather than almost anything else; coastal cities see lagged seasons. The same number runs
    the planet's thermostat — without it, daytime surfaces would roast and nights would
    freeze.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> raising temperature costs energy at a substance-specific price,
    and water's unusually high price is why oceans, kettles and coolers behave as they do.
    Next comes the strange case of energy that changes a substance without changing its
    temperature at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Latent heat: energy that changes state, not temperature</h2>
  <p>
    Keep heating boiling water and the thermometer freezes at 100 °C while the water boils
    away. The energy is still going somewhere: into breaking intermolecular bonds —
    <strong>latent heat</strong>, energy absorbed or released during a change of state with no
    temperature change. For water: melting costs 334 kJ/kg (fusion), evaporating costs a
    colossal 2,257 kJ/kg (vaporisation) — about 7 times melting, because gas molecules must
    be fully separated, not just loosened. Worked example: evaporating 1.5 kg of sweat at body
    temperature carries away 1.5 × 2,400 ≈ 3,600 kJ — enough to cool a 70 kg human by well
    over 10 °C, which is precisely how you survive exercise and why humidity (slower evaporation)
    makes heat lethal. Practical fingerprints: steam scalds worse than boiling water
    <em>because</em> it deposits its latent heat on contact; ice cools a drink both by being
    cold and by melting through those 334 kJ/kg; and frost-free freezers spend energy
    precisely to suppress the condensation–freezing cycle.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Boiling versus evaporation</strong> — related, not identical. Evaporation happens
    at any temperature, from the surface only, as the fastest molecules escape (which is why
    the puddle cools: it loses its speediest, and thus its most energetic, customers).
    Boiling happens at one temperature (where vapour pressure matches atmospheric pressure),
    throughout the liquid, in bubbles. Both cool what remains; only boiling requires the
    full latent-heat rate. A vacuum's low pressure drops the boiling point — water boils at
    body temperature near the summit of Everest, and astronauts' risk of blood boiling at
    extreme low pressure is a latent-heat fact with teeth.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pair the property — evaporation or boiling?" widget="match" config={thBoilMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> steam at 100 °C and boiling water at 100 °C scald
  equally, since they share a temperature. Steam scalds far worse: when it condenses on skin
  it deposits its latent heat of vaporisation — about 2,257 kJ/kg on top of whatever the
  hot liquid itself transfers. Same temperature, very different energy delivery.</p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="true or false — steam, sweat and ice" widget="judge" config={thSteamJudge} />

  <h2>How heat travels: conduction, convection, radiation</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Conduction</strong> moves energy through contact without moving matter: hot
    particles vibrate harder and jostle their neighbours down the line. Metals conduct
    brilliantly for a second reason — their free electrons (the same crowd that carries
    current, per the electricity chapters) ferry energy at speed, which is why the metal
    spoon in your tea handle burns while the wooden one doesn't and why both sit at the same
    temperature. Conductors: copper, aluminium. Insulators: trapped air (foam, fleece, double
    glazing — still air is the star insulator, which is why jumping mice survive winters).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Convection</strong> moves energy by moving <em>matter</em> — only possible in
    fluids: heat a region, it expands and becomes less dense, buoyancy floats it, cooler fluid
    sinks to replace it, and the loop is a convection current. Radiators heat rooms by setting
    up exactly this circulation (heat does not "rise" — <em>hot fluid</em> rises; radiation
    travels in straight lines in any direction). Weather is convection at planetary scale:
    sea breezes (land heats faster by day, air over it rises, sea air flows in), thunderstorm
    updrafts, and the mantle's slow conveyor beneath the tectonic plates.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="rebuild the sea-breeze loop — convection by the coast" widget="order" config={thBreezeOrder} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "heat rises, which is why the upstairs flat is warmer."
  Heat is energy in transit, and energy does not have a preferred direction; what rises is
  the <em>heated fluid</em>, because buoyancy floats the less dense region. Put the same
  observation in a zero-gravity cabin — or in a solid, where nothing flows — and "heat
  rises" makes no prediction at all, while convection theory predicts when it will and
  will not.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>Radiation</strong> needs no medium at all: all objects emit infrared
    electromagnetic waves, hotter objects much more (power ∝ T⁴ in kelvin — the physics the
    greenhouse effect and thermal cameras run on). Matte black surfaces are the best
    emitters <em>and</em> absorbers; shiny surfaces reflect. The Sun heats Earth through
    150 million km of vacuum this way, and every thermal camera image is a radiation map.
  </p>
  <p>
    Design consequences combine all three. The <strong>vacuum flask</strong> is a museum of
    this chapter: vacuum stops conduction and convection (no medium), silvered walls reflect
    radiation, and a stopper traps the last path. House insulation, car cooling fins,
    thermos cookers, astronaut suits (reflective layers against radiation, insulation against
    conduction, fans to move internal heat by convection where vacuum forbids it) — each is
    the three transfer mechanisms audited and blocked, one by one.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A bathtub at 30 °C and a match flame: which contains more thermal energy, and which has the higher temperature?</li>
    <li>A 2 kW kettle holds 1.5 kg of water at 20 °C. Roughly how long to reach 100 °C?</li>
    <li>A vacuum flask must defeat all three transfer mechanisms. Name the design feature that defeats each.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The bathtub — far more mass at a decent temperature means
    vastly more thermal energy — but only the flame has the higher temperature, and heat
    flows from hot to cold regardless of quantity. (2) E = mcΔθ = 1.5 × 4,186 × 80 ≈ 500 kJ;
    at 2,000 J/s that is about 250 seconds, a bit over four minutes — the lesson's 3 kW
    kettle managed 2.8 minutes. (3) The vacuum stops conduction and convection (no medium to
    conduct or circulate), the silvered walls reflect radiation, and the stopper blocks the
    last path at the top.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Temperature measures average particle kinetic energy; heat is energy in transit because of a temperature difference.</li>
    <li>One kinetic model explains solids, liquids and gases — and Brownian motion made the invisible motion visible.</li>
    <li>The gas laws come from molecular bombardment; the Kelvin scale (K = °C + 273.15) is the one the laws demand — always convert.</li>
    <li>Specific heat capacity ΔE = mcΔθ prices a degree; water's huge price runs civilisation's climate control.</li>
    <li>Latent heat changes state, not temperature: melting 334 kJ/kg, evaporating 2,257 kJ/kg for water.</li>
    <li>Conduction (contact), convection (moving matter), radiation (no medium needed) — hot fluid rises, heat does not.</li>
    <li>Good thermal design is an audit: block all three transfer paths, one by one.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling lesson on waves and sound picks up the wave side of radiation — light and
    infrared are electromagnetic waves, and the same ideas return in optics. The advanced
    lesson on statistical mechanics then rebuilds this chapter from the bottom up: what
    entropy is, why the Maxwell–Boltzmann tail exists, and where the second law's arrow of
    time comes from.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The second law and the engine ceiling.</strong> The laws ranked by audacity: the
    <strong>zeroth</strong> makes thermometers possible (things in contact reach one
    temperature); the <strong>first</strong> is energy conservation — heat in = internal
    energy gained + work done, no engine creates energy; the <strong>second</strong> says heat
    flows hot to cold spontaneously and <em>no engine can be perfect</em>: some heat must be
    dumped in a cold sink. The Carnot limit — maximum efficiency = 1 − T_cold/T_hot (in
    kelvin) — caps even ideal engines: a coal plant with steam at 810 K rejecting to 300 K
    surroundings cannot beat 1 − 300/810 ≈ 63%, and real plants achieve 35–45%. The third
    law forbids reaching absolute zero, and every lab that gets within a billionth of a
    degree agrees. Entropy — the accounting behind the second law — is the only fundamental
    law with an arrow of time: dropped eggs do not unscramble, not because energy forbids it
    but because the unscrambled arrangement is one configuration among astronomically many.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Not all molecules are average.</strong> "Temperature = average kinetic energy"
    hides a distribution: at any instant some molecules crawl and a few sprint at triple the
    average. That <strong>Maxwell–Boltzmann tail</strong> is why evaporation happens at all
    below boiling (the sprinters escape), why chemical reactions speed up steeply with
    temperature (more molecules clear the activation hurdle — the chemistry kinetics chapter
    runs on this), and why the atmosphere's lightest gases (hydrogen, helium) leak slowly
    into space: at the top of the distribution, a few atoms exceed escape velocity.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>When the ideal gas fails.</strong> The model assumes point particles with no
    mutual attraction. Push a gas toward condensation and both assumptions break — molecules
    occupy volume and attract each other, and the corrected (van der Waals) equation captures
    liquefaction. The failure is productive: it marks exactly where the liquid state begins,
    and navigating it is the science of refrigeration, of liquifying air to harvest nitrogen
    and oxygen, and of the supercritical CO₂ that decaffeinates your coffee. Even a "simple"
    law like pV = nRT has a border, and the border is another chapter's territory.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The quantum lurking in a stove.</strong> Nineteenth-century physics could predict
    almost nothing about why hot objects glow the colour they do — the classical calculation
    of radiation from a warm body predicted infinite energy (the "ultraviolet catastrophe").
    Planck fixed it in 1900 by assuming energy comes in packets — quanta — and quantum theory
    fell out of the thermal physics of glowing coals. Every thermal radiator, every star's
    colour-temperature (the Sun's 5,772 K surface glows white), and the cosmic microwave
    background's 2.7 K hiss are quantum predictions wearing a thermodynamics coat. Thermal
    physics is not a backwater; it is where the twentieth century began.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, College Physics (ch. 11–13, Temperature, Kinetic Theory, Heat Transfer); Feynman Lectures Vol. I, ch. 39–45; NIST steam tables (latent heats); Einstein (1905) on Brownian motion; Planck (1900) black-body radiation. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
